.TH GETS 3
.SH NAME
gets, fgets \- get a line from a stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <stdio.h>

char *
gets(s)
char *s;

char *
fgets(s, n, stream)
char *s;
int n;
FILE *stream;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gets
reads characters from
.B stdin
into the array
.I s
up to a newline, which it replaces with a NUL.
.PP
.B fgets
reads characters from
.I stream
into the array
.I s
until it has read a newline, which it keeps, or
.IR n \-1
characters, or comes to the end of the file, and ends them with a NUL.
.SH "RETURN VALUES"
Both return
.IR s ,
or NULL at the end of the file, or after an error, when no characters
were read.
.SH SEE ALSO
getc(3), puts(3), scanf(3), ferror(3), stdio(3)
.SH HISTORY
The
.B fgets
function first appeared in Version 7 AT&T UNIX, which also has
.BR gets .
.SH BUGS
.B gets
cannot know the length of
.IR s :
a line longer than the array overwrites whatever follows it. Use
.B fgets
on stdin when the input could be anything.
.PP
The two functions differ in what they do with the newline.
